GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17226-   "id": "sh4iprbcd",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17227-   "title": "sh4iprbcd  IPRB/IPRC/IPRD are stored by the device and read by nothing -- WDT, REF, SCI, GPIO, DMAC and the IRL lines are priority 0 permanently",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17228-   "state": "closed",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17229-   "commits": "7e5c0f6 bb05ce7",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17230-   "hold": "",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17231-   "witness": "boot:landisk (rung 4) + probe:regress/sh_iprbcd_probe.py (rung 3, 14 rows, SHIPPED). The boot witness is load-bearing: an instrumented 90s `-E landisk` boot of openbsd76-landisk-bsd.rd shows the guest writing IPRC=0x00c0 (SCIF priority 12) as `scif0: cons` attaches, while the model delivered priority 3 on all fifteen SCIF asserts. Probe measured 6/10 pre-fix (vector 1,1,1,0,0,1,0,1,0,1, reproduced identically twice) and 14/14 post-fix, with a LIVENESS control (0x12345678 through a guest store) and a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control (IPRD reset value 0xda74 via P4) green in both directions. NOTE the `naive` row -- write one IPR, expect delivery -- is GREEN pre-fix and ships as a LABELLED row rather than a witness; what discriminates is IMASK BRACKETING.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17232-   "closure": {
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17233-    "by": "fable5-mainloop",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17234-    "badge": "A",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:17235:    "note": "*** THE ROW'S TITLE NAMES IPRD; THE ROUND DECLINES TO READ IT, AND THAT IS THE FIX, NOT A RESIDUAL. *** Not-reading is the correct model: IPRD is SH7750S/SH7750R only (Rev.7.00 note 3, :39881 and :40248) while machine_dreamcast.c:165 asks for plain SH7750 where the register does not exist; it governs only IRLM=1, which dev_sh4.c:2386-2388 refuses outright; module priorities come from IPRA-IPRC per programming manual 5.6; and reading it is MEASURED HARM -- one guest `IPRD := 0`, the exact store OpenBSD/landisk 7.6 makes at boot, drops IRQ2/5/8/11 from 0xd/0xa/0x7/0x4 to zero and masks four PCIC lines, because IPRD's four nibbles ALIAS those slots (sh4_exception.h:137-140 vs :144-154). A row held open demanding a fix that would be a defect is queue pollution, so it closes.\n*** REOPEN TRIPWIRE, written now so it is not reconstructed later: IF THE IRLM=1 REFUSAL IS EVER LIFTED, THIS CLOSURE IS VOID. *** landisk's SH7751R does have IPRD, so implementing IRLM=1 makes declining it wrong on the same day; IPRD reading must land in that same commit, gated on the live IRLM bit, with an explicit policy for the IRQ2/5/8/11 aliasing -- and `iprd hold` flips from DEFENDING the decline to BLOCKING the feature, so its oracle must be re-pinned FIRST under the oracle-correction rule. The two decisions are cross-referenced in both files. Filed as sh4irlm.\nDETECTOR: the decision was defended only by a comment until the ask-again. `iprd hold`/`iprd ctl` now pin it -- MEASURED, `m_iprdread` (apply IPRD's nibbles to IRL0-3) scores 13/14, red on `iprd hold` alone, with the out-of-IPRD control green. Two further mutants (drop the RTC+IPRB mapping; drop the three RTC lines) had each scored a CLEAN 10/10 against every behavioural row, because WDT/REF/SCI/GPIO/DMAC/H-UDI/RTC have NO INTERRUPT_ASSERT in this tree; the settings exposure of int_prio_and_pending[] exists so two rows can read the TABLE, and it closes them exactly.\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM: `scifonly` and `ipronly` build byte-for-byte identical binaries, so the entire behaviourally-witnessable payload is the SCIF re-source. The other nibbles make the model's table match the manual for sources nothing can currently raise -- worth doing, but a judgement about scope, not a measurement. Escapes FILED as sh4iprunpinned (eight shapes, five seats)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17236-   },
