GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17788-     "phase": "assess",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17789-     "d": "✓",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17790-     "date": "2026-08-23",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17791-     "note": "*** THE ROW SAYS 'NOT WITNESSED', AND A WITNESS IS BOTH CONSTRUCTIBLE AND ADMISSIBLE. *** The carrier's own rule is that 'no witness of either kind' is a CHECKABLE claim and must be checked rather than asserted -- dfreq's was measured false by building one in a single session -- so this is that check, done by READING (flagged as a reading, not a measurement; the tree was busy with #461's pre-fix run and a probe would have contended with it). THE OBSERVABLE ALREADY EXISTS: cpu_sh.c:168-173 registers `iprio_%x` as a readable setting for EVERY 0x20 slot in 0..0xfff, which includes `iprio_3e0` (IRQ15). The hardwired priority loop at cpu_sh.c:390 is `for (i = SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0; i <= SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14; i += 0x20)`, so slot 0x3e0 is never written and keeps 0 while 0x3c0 (IRQ14) gets 1. Two `print` commands on an unmodified machine separate the two. AND RUNG 2 IS THE RIGHT RUNG FOR IT, not a concession: the ladder licenses machine construction as a reproduction for 'defects in code construction itself executes (init-time)', naming the macppc heap OOB #23 as the exemplar. This IS init-time -- the loop populates the priority table at CPU init and the defect is a slot it never reaches. The ladder's warning about rung 2 is that registration proves presence not reachability, for ACCESS-PATH defects; this is not one. WHAT A RUNG-2 WITNESS WOULD NOT SETTLE, said plainly: it observes the priority TABLE, not delivery. It cannot show that an asserted line 15 is dropped -- for that the carrier's free-running shape is required (sr=0x40000000, vbr into RAM, `bra .` after the trigger, breakpoint at VBR+0x600), and driving a PCIC line needs a PCI device landing on line 15, since dev_sh4.c:2951-2967 registers the 16 lines as `<path>.pcic.<i>` for bus_pci_init's pciirq rather than as anything a guest store reaches directly. So the honest split is: the priority-0 STATE is witnessable at rung 2 today; the DROPPED-DELIVERY symptom is not, and the round should not claim it is. STALE CITATION CORRECTED: the row cites dev_sh4.c:2822-2836 for the wiring; that range is now the default arm's fatal()/exit block. The wiring is at :2951-2967."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17792-    }
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17793-   ],
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17794-   "state_note": "*** FIRST EVIDENCE 2026-08-27. The row was a BARE TITLE with an empty note; a farmed codex job established it and every load-bearing claim was re-verified here by reading. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE.** `dev_sh4.c` registers SIXTEEN PCIC interrupts (the *'Register 16 PCIC interrupts'* loop over `N_PCIC_IRQS`), and `SH4_INTEVT_IRQ15` is `0x3e0` (`sh4_exception.h:157`). But priority initialisation in `cpu_sh.c` runs\n\n    for (i = SH4_INTEVT_IRQ0; i <= SH4_INTEVT_IRQ14; i += 0x20)\n\n**-- it stops at IRQ14.** Line 15 therefore keeps priority 0, and an assertion at priority 0 returns without scheduling delivery. **A line the device registers can never be delivered.**\n\n**IT IS LATENT, and the seat established that rather than asserting it:** PCIC MMIO never generates an interrupt; no modelled device connects to `.pcic.15`; landisk's only prospective PCI device is compile-disabled AND would use line 5 anyway. **There is no guest access sequence in the shipped tree**, so this ranks below anything guest-reachable.\n\n**THE OBSERVABLE a detector would key on:** guest interrupt-vector entry with `SH4_INTEVT == 0x3e0` -- delivery sets INTEVT and transfers to `VBR+0x600`; the broken code never reaches that handler. That is one channel, not a design.\n\n*The seat labelled its own work honestly: 'MEASURED -- Not run; no build is needed to settle these source facts.' Correct, and it is why this row could be settled under the machine embargo.*\n\n*** ONE VERIFICATION NOTE, because it cuts against my own checking rather than the seat's: my grep for the literal `0x3e0` in the device file returned NOTHING and I nearly scored the citation as wrong. The file uses the SYMBOL `SH4_INTEVT_IRQ15`. The seat cited the right site; my pattern could not see it. Same shape as the other apparatus misses today -- searching for a value where the code names a constant.***\n\nNOT FIXED: extending the loop to IRQ15 is a one-token change, but it is an EDIT TO INTERRUPT PRIORITY INITIALISATION and the addition licence wants a measured delta. With no device on line 15 there is nothing to measure today -- which is precisely why the row is latent, and why the fix should ride with the round that first connects a device there.\n\n"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17795-  },
