GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17876-     "seat": "fable5",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17877-     "phase": "review",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17878-     "d": "✓",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17879-     "date": "2026-08-23",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17880-     "note": "Held out of #459 as a CAPABILITY change in a different file and a different semantic class, per the round-scope rule -- two seats proposed it and two refused the merge. THE ARGUMENT FOR: baresh and testsh are SYNTHETIC machines, so their parameters are the project's own definition rather than a hardware fact; assigning a pclock invents nothing, and it is the only way the tree gets a machine on which a future TMU round can build a witness at all. THE ARGUMENT AGAINST: testsh's value today is precisely as the regression case for 'an arch-4 CPU whose board omitted pclock', and giving it a clock deletes the only test of the missing-clock path -- so any round doing this MUST keep one machine clockless (baresh or hpcsh) and say which. A seat MEASURED 2^25 = 33554432 working, chosen because every TMU prescaler (/4, /16, /64, /256) divides it exactly and nobody will mistake it for a Jornada measurement; it produces 8388608.0 Hz at /4."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-17881-    }
