GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18500-     "date": "2026-08-23",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18501-     "note": "*** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window's +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect's actual content -- clobbering a value register's upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief's 'SH-4 can run either endianness' is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing's own 'real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits' was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match, but *** IT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE *** -- its warrant is 'a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so the latch costs a correct guest nothing', and that warrant FAILS here. Decided design recorded as B': decline at the four VALUE registers only, keep +0x18 byte-capable. It also flagged that the row pinning the REAL contract is currently UNBUILDABLE -- any nonzero store at +0x18 hits one of sh4gdromexit's filed exit(1) kills -- so this item is gated behind that round by probe-buildability rather than by ranking."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18502-    }
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18503-   ],
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18504-   "state_note": "*** A FARMED agy JOB RE-DERIVED THIS ROW INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-26, AND ITS RECOMMENDATION WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A DEFECT. Recorded because the failure mode is instructive, not because the seat was careless. ***\n\n**What it got RIGHT, and it is a clean confirmation:** the tail store is `d->dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata;` with **no `len`**, `idata` arrives zero-extended from `memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)`, an aligned narrow write to an IMPLEMENTED offset therefore widens to 32 bits, and it is directly guest-reachable. It gave a correct three-way oracle table (defect `0x000000ff` / merged `0x112233ff` / declined `0x11223344`) and named both controls.\n\n**What it MISSED is what the source already says at the store itself, and it is decisive:**\n  1. **THE ORACLE IS ABSENT.** `0x005f74xx` is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it. *'Real hardware preserves the upper bits'* would be an ORACLE FROM PLAUSIBILITY -- the `#357`/`#362` shape this tree has already been burned by once.\n  2. *** MAINLINE LINUX CONSTRAINS ANY FIX, and the comment records that this is the finding which KILLED THE PANEL'S UNANIMOUS ANSWER. *** `drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c` BYTE-WRITES `+0x18` to trigger every DMA transfer and BYTE-READS it to poll for completion. **So the width-blind code delivers exactly what the one real narrow user needs, and a blanket 'decline narrow writes' would BREAK A BOOTING LINUX.** The agy answer cited that same driver as EVIDENCE FOR the defect while missing that it is the CONSTRAINT AGAINST the obvious fix.\n  3. **The detector has a trap already named:** the discriminating row must NOT ship pinning `0x000000ff`, because that would enshrine this model's own behaviour as an oracle.\n\n*** AND THE PROVENANCE CLAIM WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH RECORDING: the seat labelled its oracle table MEASURED. *** Its numbers -- `0x000000ff`, liveness `0x12345678` -- are the EXACT values recorded in the source comment, which documents a real earlier measurement. **The numbers are true; the seat did not produce them.** That is the reading-vs-measurement distinction this project draws, and it matters because a seat quoting the tree's own measurement back looks exactly like independent confirmation.\n\n**DISPOSITION UNCHANGED: assessed-not-changed.** The decided design, if a fix is ever warranted, is already written at the store: decline with a latched diagnostic at the four VALUE registers ONLY, keeping `+0x18` byte-capable. The farmed job confirmed the reading and did not advance the row.\n\n*Worth carrying about the farm generally: a seat given a row whose analysis is already COMPLETE IN THE SOURCE will re-derive it and can reach a WORSE conclusion, because the constraints live in the comment rather than in the code it reads. Brief a seat with the existing record, or ask it a question the record does not already answer.*\n\nASSESSED-NOT-CHANGED under Option C. Code ships under this row's name at ba30e64's successor -- a documenting comment at the tail store -- but the defect is deliberately unfixed, so the row stays open. Gated behind sh4gdromexit by PROBE-BUILDABILITY, not priority: the row that would pin the real contract is unbuildable until that round removes the exit(1) kills at +0x18.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18505-   "note": "  *** CITATIONS RE-DERIVED 2026-08-31 AND TWO ARE STALE. ***  This row cites `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:515` and `:889`; today those lines are bare comment continuation (`*` and `*  Mount:`) and hold no code at all.  The construct the row is about -- the DMA tail store that ignores `len` -- now sits beside the comment *\"THEN the tail stores it into dma_reg[1].  The same alias exists at\"*.  **DURABLE ANCHOR, replacing both numbers: `grep -n 'tail stores it into dma_reg' src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`.**  A grep anchor that matches nothing is LOUD; a drifted line number is silent and still looks like a citation.  *The file was committed five times after this note was written, which is exactly the window the citation-form rule describes: a same-file line number is invalidated by the round's own later edits, and nobody re-checks an example once it is written down.*"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18506-  },
