GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18530-     "d": "✓",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18531-     "date": "2026-08-24",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18532-     "note": "PASS 1, nine seats. *** MY PREMISE 1 WAS REFUTED BY GIT, NOT MERELY OVER-READ. *** The brief claimed Site A is 'already half-fixed' because the read branch's exit(1) is commented out while the write branch's is live, and inferred someone had decided the kill was wrong. `git log -L 676,700:src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` returns EXACTLY ONE COMMIT -- the unmodified upstream import. This fork never touched those lines. The true explanation is a BETTER argument: GDROM_STAT three lines later has its commented-out exit in the OPPOSITE (write) branch, so it is a REACHABILITY TRACE -- upstream commented out whichever exit he happened to hit against one driver. The live kill survived because that driver never wrote a non-0xb value, not because such writes are unthinkable. Three seats, three levels: one accepted the premise, one called the inference weak, one ran git log -L. PREMISE 5 ALSO WRONG: removing these guards does NOT delete the announcement -- fatal() remains and keeps logging; only process termination goes. The de-escalation cost is therefore lower than #447/#462, where the fix converted a loud kill into a SILENTLY refused write. DETECTOR TRAPS: do NOT pin d->stat (reading +0x8c calls update_stat which overwrites it unconditionally -- born vacuous); and every accept row here asserts ABSENCE of a diagnostic at a warn-once site, so a decline row running earlier in the same session sets the latch, suppresses the growth mutant's diagnostic, and leaves the accept row GREEN under its own mutant -- each accept row needs its OWN session, which cuts against the probe's one-session rule and the round must say which rule governs which row. SITE A HAS NO STATE CHANNEL: its write branch contains no store even for the legal 0xb, so only an absence-of-diagnostic row is available. PREREQUISITE: gdromwarnbits' pre-set trigger FIRES HERE -- 6 of 8 bits used, uint8_t, and this round needs 3 more (Site A's read branch is an unlatched flood today). 1u<<8 truncates to 0 and the diagnostic floods, so the enum-of-bit-positions conversion is due IN-ROUND, not filed."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18533-    },
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18534-    {
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18535-     "seat": "grok",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18536-     "phase": "review",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18537-     "d": "✓",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18538-     "date": "2026-08-24",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18539:     "note": "*** THIS ID NAMED TWO DIFFERENT DEFECTS, AND CLOSING IT ON ONE WOULD HAVE HIDDEN THE OTHER. *** The ledger title describes the BADVAL kills at COND and UNKNOWN_0x88. But OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:7575-7587 -- which is what the NAME describes -- records a different defect entirely: after #460, COND_DATA_AVAIL PERMANENTLY LIES once a guest reads past the buffer, the device advertising that data is available while returning 0xffff for every subsequent read. That is a STATE REPAIR with its own oracle; it does not join the BADVAL round. Split into gdromcondavail so neither can be closed by work on the other -- the dishonest-listing class #270 exists to prevent exactly this. Caught by a seat auditing the brief's premises."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18540-    },
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18541-    {
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18542-     "seat": "fable5",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18543-     "phase": "review",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18544-     "d": "✓",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18545-     "date": "2026-08-24",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18546-     "note": "PASS 1, FLAGSHIP SEAT. *** THIS ENTRY EXISTED ONLY AS PROSE UNTIL PRECOMMIT SECTION I BLOCKED THE COMMIT. *** The seat answered in full; its findings were written into OUTSTANDING_BUGS and never recorded as a ledger entry, so the row read as '2 seats and NO flagship seat'. A seat that answered and was not recorded is the same defect class as a seat that did not answer -- which is why the gate cannot tell them apart and correctly refuses. FINDINGS: (1) my brief said 'quoted verbatim' over a THIRTEEN-line comment reproduced as TWO, with no ellipsis. (2) Q2 is settled by in-tree precedent, not unknowable -- dev_wdc.c, the tree's OTHER ATA/ATAPI device, survives an unimplemented command (error |= WDCE_ABRT + fatal, NO exit), and 0xef IS SET FEATURES; but do NOT port the ABRT bits, because this model has no error register and update_stat() regenerates stat on every read, so a latched error would be overwritten -- the route has nowhere to live. (3) The sharpest argument for 'leave d->cond unchanged' is not the hazard but that THERE IS NO BEHAVIOUR TO PRESERVE: pre-fix the store is observable only for ACCEPTED values because every declined path exits, so no surviving execution has ever observed a stored illegal value. (4) Site A CAN carry a failable accept row after all -- content-keyed diagnostic ABSENCE with an in-session positive control -- but only if its diagnostic gains the VALUE (%x), else a growth mutant makes the legal 0xb store consume the once-latch and print the SAME text a control would, leaving count-keyed rows green. (5) The gdromwarnbits pre-set trigger fires in THAT round: 6 of 8 bits used, uint8_t, and it needs 2-3 more."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18547-    },
