GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18851-     "outcome_note": "[HELD] regress review of a closure made from outside the shipping round",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18852:     "note": "[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  Regress review of the CLOSURE, not of the fix.  **This is a hold, not a seat failure and not agreement.**  *** THE REASON IT NEEDS ONE: I closed this row on inference from commit messages, days after the round that shipped the fix, and a HIGH row closed by a third party is the closure most likely to be wrong in a way nobody notices -- the shipping round would have known its own scope; I reconstructed it. ***  The specific risk is the SPLIT: this id was divided, with `gdromcondavail` taking the other defect, and #496 relocated a store on the shared `d->cond` byte that both rows touch.  *A closure that is right about its own pair can still be wrong about what it implies for its sibling.*"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18853-    }
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18854-   ],
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18855-   "sev": "high",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18856-   "note": "*** WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-08-30, BEFORE ANY EDIT.  Both kills reproduce on the committed build. ***  One guest `mov.w` per site, planted in RAM and executed through real decode and real `memory_rw` on an unmodified `-E dreamcast`.  Exact observable: `waitpid` reports `WIFEXITED`, status 1 -- the `exit(1)` at `:1094` and `:1154`; the debugger prompt never returns.  Built from `git archive` into `/tmp`, so nothing was written to the repo and NO `.MUTANT` was owed; two-part clearance RUN, not asserted.  *** THE DEVICE-SIGNATURE CONTROL IS THE STRONG FORM, and it is worth copying: a TRANSFORMATION ONLY THE DEVICE PERFORMS. ***  The write arm masks to a byte, so storing `0x1234` to `CNTLO` reads back `0x34`.  RAM would return `0x1234` verbatim, so this discriminates against a RAM-BACKED WINDOW as well as against a dead one -- and it sits in the same register file as both kills.  A value-only control cannot do that; this project's footbridge probe once returned 0x0 everywhere with its RAM control green.  *** AND IT CARRIES ACCEPT ROWS THE LADDER DOES NOT REQUIRE BUT THIS RESULT NEEDS: `0xb` survives at site A, `0xa0` and `0xef` at site B. ***  Without them the measurement is equally consistent with the offset being UNSERVICEABLE -- which is the already-fixed `#461` default-arm defect, a different bug.  **The accept rows are what pin it to the VALUE**, and therefore what make this BADVAL (`#443`) rather than an unimplemented-offset kill.  ATTRIBUTION CONTROL, also unrequested: each kill row is paired with that site's own diagnostic, and site B's checks the echoed byte TRACKS the guest datum (`0x55`->`55`, `0x01`->`01`).  That proves the guest's value reached the predicate rather than merely that something died -- and it makes the patterns failable in both directions, since the same pattern reads 1 on a kill session and 0 on an accept one.  *** CLOSED 2026-08-30.  THE FIX HAD ALREADY SHIPPED AND THIS ROW NAMED NO COMMIT -- IT STOOD AT sev=high, state=`in work`, `commits` EMPTY, SO THE QUEUE ADVERTISED AN UNFIXED CRITICAL BUG THAT NO LONGER EXISTED. ***  `c516a9c` is titled *\"#496 GD-ROM: two guest-reachable host kills de-fatalised\"* and its body names EXACTLY this row's pair -- `GDROM_UNKNOWN_0x88` (write of any value but 0x0b) and `GDROM_COND` (write of anything outside {0xa0, 0xef}) -- calling them *\"the #443 BADVAL class\"*, which is the class this row's own title scopes itself to (\"BADVAL PAIR ONLY\").  Follow-ups `439dfd0` and `105c15c` close two measured escapes and land the detector's first clean baselines.  **AND THE FIX WAS LARGER THAN DE-FATALISATION, which is why closing it on the exit() count alone would have been wrong:** `d->cond = idata;` ran UNCONDITIONALLY above the dispatch, and COND_DATA_AVAIL is a bit of that byte gating the whole GDROM_DATA read path -- so deleting the `exit(1)` alone would have handed every unmodelled value a direct write to the readiness flag the guest polls.  Silent, and worse than the kill it replaced.  The store now lives inside the two accepted arms.  **THE WITNESS STAYS AND IS THE ROW'S BEST ARTIFACT** -- rung 3, 15/15 pre-fix, built BEFORE any edit, both kills reproduced on the committed build via one guest `mov.w` each through real decode on an unmodified `-E dreamcast`.  Test-first, done in the right order.  *** BUT ITS LINE CITATIONS HAVE GONE STALE, WHICH IS HOW THIS WAS FOUND. ***  The witness names the kills at `:1094` and `:1154`; today those lines are the `#460` and `#496` COMMENTS that replaced them, and the file's only live `exit(1)` is at `:1108` -- a different defect entirely (`cmd_count >= 12`, a COUNT rather than a VALUE, and outside this row's scope).  **A reader checking the row by opening its cited lines would have found comments where kills were claimed and drawn the opposite conclusion.**  That is the `citestale` class exactly -- a same-file citation invalidated by the round's own later edits -- and it is why the citation-form rule prefers grep anchors to line numbers.  *Found by reading the file rather than the row: the queue said HIGH and unfixed, the source said otherwise, and the commit log settled it.*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18857-   "state_note": "  *** PASS 1 FIRED 2026-08-30, panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260830_153427`.  NINE SEATS FIRED, NINE ANSWERED -- seven scriptable plus both Agent seats.  NOTHING DOWN. ***  `ollama_kimi` writes no file BY DESIGN since 2026-08-29: the kimi CLI is the seat again and `OLLAMA4` is deliberately blank, so its absence is the CONFIGURED STATE and not a lost seat -- a check that counts files in the panel dir will under-count here, and separately under-counts by two because the Agent seats leave no file at all.  kimi is recorded PARTIAL (still writing at 230 KB when the entries were written).  *** THE ROSTER SENTENCE LIVES HERE, ON THE ROW, BECAUSE IT IS A FACT ABOUT THE PANEL AND NOT ABOUT ANY SEAT. ***  It was first written as a stamped header on all nine seat entries and `check_boilerplate.py` refused the commit -- correctly, and the refusal is worth recording rather than just fixing: **a note shared verbatim by several seats records that they were FIRED, not that they AGREED**, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape arriving in the records instead of in the panel.  Nine identical prefixes would have rendered as nine seats saying the same thing, on the very round whose two Agent seats DISAGREED about scope.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18858-   "closed": "2026-08-30",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18859:   "hold_question": "I closed this row from OUTSIDE the round that fixed it -- by reading `c516a9c`'s commit message and the source, days later -- so the closure rests on my inference rather than on the shipping round's own record.  That is the closure most worth a second reading.  **Q1: does #496 actually cover this row's WHOLE scope?**  The title scopes it to the \"BADVAL PAIR ONLY\" and explicitly hands a second defect to `gdromcondavail` -- so a closure that is right about the pair could still be wrong if #496 touched shared code in a way that changes the other row's status, in either direction.  **Q2: is `gdromcondavail` still live and still correctly scoped after #496?**  It says COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread; #496 MOVED the `d->cond = idata;` store inside the two accepted arms, and COND_DATA_AVAIL is a bit of that same byte.  A relocation of the store is exactly the kind of edit that can fix or worsen a neighbouring row silently.  **Q3: the witness's line citations are STALE** (`:1094`/`:1154` are now the comments that replaced the kills, and the file's only live `exit(1)` is `:1108`, a different defect).  Should the witness field be re-pointed at grep anchors, or is a stale citation on a CLOSED row acceptable because the artifact itself is preserved?"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-18860-  },
