GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-20459-   "state_note": "*** THE FABLE PASS-2 SEAT FOUND WHAT NEITHER I NOR THE CODEX SEAT SAW: THE 'OWED' ROWS WERE ALREADY BUILT AND MEASURED, IN THIS ROUND'S OWN COMMITTED KILL TABLE. *** Verified here by reading `killtable_20260826/gxo465_det_*.txt`: the 26-row instrument carries an `E mism|rej how/val/arm/cnt pre/cnt post` family -- **those ARE the `:406`/`:415` tail-reach rows** -- scored against all eight builds. So 'owed coverage debt' understated the round's own evidence. *** AND THE DECISIVE ROW KILLS BOTH RETURN-MUTANTS, WHICH SHARPENS THE FINDING: *** `E:mism cnt post` (\"TAIL-REACH: :415 must fall INTO the tail, which zeroes cnt\") reads 0x0==0x0 ok on base, and **0x1 vs 0x0 FAIL on BOTH `m_ret415` AND `m_helper_ret`.** It is a structural tail-reach oracle, not a form-specific one -- it detects the bypass whichever fix design is in place. AND IT SUPPLIES THE LICENCE DELTA THE ROUND'S RECORD CITED WRONGLY: `E:mism how` is **sig11 on `m_ret415`** (the defect measurably RETURNS under the rejected tail form) and **exit0 on `m_helper_ret`** (it does not under the shipped helper). Same one-word edit, both candidate fixes, opposite outcomes -- that is the addition licence's 'measured delta' in its strongest form, and the CHANGELOG had cited a 16/16 sweep instead, which the sibling mutant reproduces identically and which therefore measures nothing about A-vs-B. *** ONE CORRECTION TO THE ADJUDICATION, and it is material to its own recommendation. *** The seat advised completing the L1 in-round on the grounds that 'the construction and measurement cost is already paid; porting is mechanical'. **THE INSTRUMENT IS GONE.** I preserved the seat's OUTPUTS (8 score logs, 8 build logs, the pristine source) and NOT the probe that produced them; WSL `/tmp` holds only `m8ctl.py`, `instr.py`, `headprobe.py`. So porting is RECONSTRUCTION FROM SPEC, not a port -- tractable, because each row's route and oracle are fully described in the output, but it is construction work with its own measurement obligations. **THE LESSON IS MINE AND IT GENERALISES: THE DURABLE-PATH RULE SAYS PRESERVE THE MEASUREMENT; THIS SHOWS IT MUST ALSO PRESERVE THE INSTRUMENT.** A score log makes a claim CHECKABLE; only the instrument makes it REPRODUCIBLE or PORTABLE. I saved 22 artifacts and missed the one that mattered most for the round's next step. Filed as `preserveinstrument`.\n\n*** PASS 2 REFUTED THIS ROUND'S OWN m_ret415 CLAIM -- TWICE, INDEPENDENTLY, FROM THE ROUND'S OWN LOGS. *** The record said `m_ret415` \"swept a clean detector 16/16\". True of the INTENT-ONLY subset only; on the FULL table it is 23/26, and it attacks the REJECTED tail-assignment design rather than the shipped helper. The mutant against the SHIPPED design is `m_helper_ret`, KILLED at 25/26; the real clause-(d) repair-null mutant is `m_swapcnt`, STRONGLY KILLED at 14/26. **So the shipped fix's own repair IS defended -- no floor breach.** Filing the missing tail rows was right; calling them L1 was not, because L1 means finish in-round, so 'L1 and omitted' was internally inconsistent. Reclassified as ABOVE-FLOOR COVERAGE DEBT. FOUR L4 WRONG RECORDS CORRECTED IN-ROUND, all mine, NONE in the C fix -- which both seats confirmed sound at both sites: (1) the probe header still described the defect in the PRESENT TENSE after the fix shipped, calling itself a WITNESS; (2) the provenance comment said 'quoted' and then PARAPHRASED, with no verbatim text and no line reference -- exactly the gap the oracle-correction rule exists to close, since the old pin's authority cannot also be the warrant; (3) the staged-route comment claimed 'NO diagnostic of any kind' directly above a row expecting one; (4) the C comment claimed the helper made the disagreeing state UNREPRESENTABLE -- an overclaim, since the fields stay independent and alloc_data() itself sets the length before the pointer. The codex seat supplied a writer the round's premise MISSED: device construction zeroes the whole struct. Safe (it sets both fields), but 'only two writers' was incomplete -- a grep is not a proof.\n\n*** #465 SHIPPED: the `free_data()` helper, Option B, with its detector wired into a gate that was running NOTHING. *** THE DEFECT: `alloc_data()` sets `data` and `data_len` together; both release sites cleared only the POINTER, leaving `data_len` describing a buffer that no longer exists. A guest PIO sequence reached it -- 9 accesses cheap route, 18 staged -- both ending in SIGSEGV of the HOST process. THE FIX and its LICENCE: a helper is an ADDITION, so the addition licence governs rather than minimal-surgical, and it clears on both grounds. MEASURED DELTA: Option A cleared data_len at the function tail, which a mid-switch `return` bypasses -- `m_ret415` (one word, `break;` -> `return;`) SWEPT a clean detector 16/16. TRANSFERRED WARRANT: `#182`'s rule that a pointer and its length are updated together. Releasing both in one place makes the disagreeing state UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely unreached. *** THE ORACLE CORRECTION RAN IN THE MANDATED ORDER, and four preserved logs prove it: *** prefix_HEAD_13of13.raw (committed, old pin, 13/13 -- defect reproduces, controls live); repin_HEAD_RED.raw (committed, NEW pin, 12/13 -- RED measured BEFORE any edit); postfix_465.raw (fixed, 11/13 -- crash gone, witness rows still pinning the defect); postfix_465_final.raw (fixed, final pin, 13/13). Same scripts, opposite colour, RED first. `staged quiet` was re-pinned 0-of-8 -> 1-of-8 because the repaired state routes into #460's once-latched overread decline -- **the fix is LOUDER by one line, and the round's draft prose claiming it 'invents no new behaviour' was false and is corrected in the probe header.** *** THE DETECTOR WAS WIRED INTO NO GATE. *** Grepping the battery for the probe's own name returned nothing: committed under regress/artifacts/ with nothing executing it, which would have made 'shipped a detector with the fix' a FALSE RECORD (L3) in a round whose subject is fields that disagree. Now a gate_sh_rounding.sh section modelled on wdc_flood -- parse before score, denominator against a literal, per-row presence -- plus a NEW guard: **CONTROLS ASSERTED SEPARATELY**, because a run of mine scored 1/13 with all five controls dead and read as catastrophic regression when the cause was a nonexistent image path I invented. Wiring it also caught a `GDLOG` collision that would have silently overwritten the gate_gdrom_data log. OWED AND NOT IN THIS COMMIT, recorded rather than quietly omitted: the `:406`/`:415` tail-reach rows as L1 -- `m_ret415` sweeps the intent detector clean, so the tail-bypass class is real coverage debt with a MEASURED escape. AND A RESULT WORTH KEEPING: `m_free` and `m_top` both score 13/16 but NOT THE SAME 13 -- each witnessed route is the sole killer of one relocation mutant, so the two are NOT redundant and a future round tempted to drop one as duplicative is measured wrong.\n\n*** ORDERING DEPENDENCY EXPORTED to `gdromexitsix` 2026-08-26 -- recorded here so a reader of this row never sees false completeness. *** This round's detector oracle is the DEATH MODE, and it depends on three command-layer `exit(1)` calls that `gdromexitsix` is scheduled to de-fatalise. That obligation now lives on THAT row as a CLOSURE-GATING re-pin, with the oracle-correction order baked in -- because the LATER round breaks the EARLIER round's detector, so it must sit where the round that will trip it will see it.\n\n  *** WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-08-25. THE DEFECT IS REAL, AND IT IS CHEAPER AND QUIETER THAN THIS ROW SAID. *** The committed build dies with **SIGSEGV on NINE guest accesses**, every value legal. A second, independent 18-access staged route fires too. *** THE ORACLE IS THE DEATH MODE, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES `died` EVIDENCE HERE. *** Every OTHER death in this device is `fatal()` + `exit(1)`; this one is a NULL deref, so the probe reports HOW the process ended and `sig11` != `exit1` != `exit0`. **That validates the hand-assembled packet for free**: a wrong `cmd[0]` lands in `handle_command`'s `default:` arm and a wrong `cmd[1]` in the 0x30 format check -- both `exit(1)` -- so a coding error in the probe CANNOT be mistaken for the defect. This project has a recorded hand-assembled-encoding trap; this probe is immune to it by construction. `grep -c 'SIGSEGV|SIGBUS'` over `src/` is 0 of 0, so nothing catches it. THE NINE ACCESSES: store 0x00a0 to COND (0xa05f709c) sets `busy |= 0x08`; six stores to DATA (0xa05f7080) spelling `30 20 ...` = read-sectors, format 0x20, sector 0, count 1; `cnt == 0` so :409 sets cnt = 2048 and `alloc_data` sets **data_len = 2048**; `diskimage_access` returns 0 non-fatally; :445-446 does `free(d->data); d->data = NULL`; **the tail zeroes `d->cnt` and clears COND_DATA_AVAIL but NOT `data_len`**; store 0x00ef to COND restores bit 3; then ONE LOAD of DATA -- `0 < 2048` is true, `d->data[0]` on NULL, SIGSEGV. *** ATTRIBUTION, BY A ONE-LINE CONTROL RATHER THAN BY ARGUMENT: *** a second scratch copy with `DIFF_LINES=1` -- one added `if (d->data == NULL) { odata = 0xffff; break; }` at :611 -- scores **11/13, red at EXACTLY `cheap trig` and `staged trig`** (sig11 -> exit0), every other row byte-identical. The kill is attributed to that deref and nothing else. *The seat was explicit that this mutant is an ATTRIBUTION CONTROL, not a fix recommendation -- the option set has three members and it did not adjudicate them.* *** THIS ROW'S OWN ESTIMATE IS CORRECTED, AND UPWARD IN PRECISION: 9 IS THE MINIMUM, DERIVED RATHER THAN GUESSED. *** `data_len` becomes nonzero only via `alloc_data`, which needs a dispatched command; dispatch needs `busy & 0x08`, set only by COND = 0xa0; the packet needs six stores (the width guard consumes 2 bytes at ANY width, so no wider store shortens it); bit 3 must be restored by the guest because the tail clears it unconditionally on the NULL branch, and **0xef is one of only TWO values GDROM_COND accepts at all** -- every other value hits `exit(1)` at :745. 1 + 6 + 1 + 1 = 9. *** AND A WRONG RECORD IN A SIBLING ENTRY IS CORRECTED: THE DEFECT IS NOT DISKLESS-ONLY. *** The opus5 entry attributes the cheap route to 'no IDE disk attached'. Re-run with a real `IDE DISK id 0, 64 MB` attached: **still 13/13**. `prom_emulation` defaults to 1 (`machine.c:85`), so :430 does `sector_nr -= 150`, sector 0 becomes -150, and the read fails on a negative offset regardless of any disk. Neither route needs the absence of a disk. ALL THREE PREMISES HOLD AND TWO ARE UNDERSTATED. (1) NO NULL TEST -- the whole arm :523-641 has exactly three guards: width (:580, which WARNS AND CONTINUES), COND_DATA_AVAIL (:590), and `cur_data_offset < data_len` (:611). `d->data` is never tested. `data_len` has exactly one writer, `alloc_data` at :307. **The DMA twin got its NULL test at :846 in #462; the PIO arm did not.** (2) LEGAL VALUES ONLY -- stronger than stated: every value is one the device explicitly models (COND 0xa0; packets 0x14 / 0x70 / 0x30 with format 0x20, i.e. NetBSD's `gdrom_read_toc`, `gdrom_mount_disk`, `gdrom_read_sectors`). No malformed packet, no bad width, no over-long command. (3) NO DIAGNOSTIC -- now MEASURED rather than read: the staged route produces **0 of 8** device diagnostics. Honest qualifier from the seat: `debug(\"[ GDROM cmd: ...\")` at :319-322 does print under stepping, but that is a COMMAND TRACE and not a warning about the pointer, which is why the counter excludes it. The cheap route IS loud -- but its noise is about the disk read and arrives two accesses BEFORE the kill. LIMITS, STATED PLAINLY BY THE SEAT: rung 3, not rung 4 -- no driver was shown to travel this path unaided; the instruction stream is planted with `put h` from the debugger, which is the shipped in-tree idiom (`regress/gdrom_data_probe.py`). No gdb backtrace was taken; attribution rests on the one-line control plus the sig11/exit1/exit0 discriminator, which the seat judged stronger than a stripped -O3 trace. TREE HYGIENE VERIFIED, and the seat caught HEAD moving under it mid-run (faffad7 -> b744299, this session): all 474 tracked `src/` files byte-identical to committed HEAD, the one dirty src file is `dev_wdc.c` and its copy holds the HEAD version. Nothing was written to GXEMUL-SEC. *It also hit the WSL arg-path trap this project records: an inline `$(wc -c ...)` reported 277 bytes for a 759-byte file. Everything load-bearing after that went through script files.*  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25 (fable5, main-loop hat). THE MECHANISM PREMISE IS CONFIRMED AT EVERY STEP, THE FIX IS MEMBER 2, AND #462 IS **NOT** A WRONG RECORD. *** **THE WRONG-RECORD QUESTION, ANSWERED NO — I asked it and the answer is that #462 scoped itself honestly.** Its CHANGELOG carries a dedicated section *\"### Scope: the DMA pair only\"* stating that the four command-layer exits do not join on different reach and a different fix idiom, and the `gdromexitsix` row's own TITLE says *\"the DMA+0x18 pair ... and the four command-layer exits split out\"*. Neither claims to have repaired the `data`/`data_len` invariant generally. **The PIO read handler is a THIRD site** — neither the DMA arm nor one of the four command-layer exits — and #462 was right not to join it: reaching it needs a `COND=0xa0` plus six-store packet sequence, versus single-window DMA stores. *That is the reopening rule working as designed.* The carrier's clause-(d) founding case cites this same field pair, but that citation is about #462's DETECTOR being blind to an operand swap on the DMA guard — which #462's final detector then killed at 56/58. It was never a claim to have fixed the invariant. **No correction owed.** *** THE DECISIVE FACT MY OPTION SET MISSED, FOUND BY GREPPING THE FORK AS THE THREE-MEMBERED RULE DIRECTS: THE PIO READ ALREADY HAS A SHIPPED, REVIEWED DECLINE PATH. *** At :625-641, the `else` of the :611 guard is **#460's over-read handler — warn-once, `odata = 0xffff`, break** — annotated as this device's own idiom for *\"read when there is nothing to give\"*. **MEMBER 2 IS THE FIX, and its best form is not the one I proposed.** One line in the TAIL ELSE-BRANCH beside the existing `d->cnt = 0` at :481 — **NOT at the free sites**. The derived argument, which is a correctness argument and not a preference: `d->data` is NULLed only inside `handle_command` (:326 top, :446 on read failure), and **every such path falls through to the unconditional tail whose else-branch runs iff `data == NULL`** — so ONE assignment maintains the invariant on ALL NULL-leaving paths, where a free-site reset needs two and the :326 one is dead in the common realloc case. It co-locates invariant maintenance with the sibling `cnt` zeroing already there. **And it carries a TRANSFERRED WARRANT: with `data_len = 0` the PIO read's `0 < 0` is false and the access routes into #460's existing decline — no new behaviour, no new decline site, and LOUDER than a NULL test's silent 0xffff, because it fires the #460 diagnostic once.** Every consumer of the pair benefits, including the DMA arm: its `data == NULL` guard and a hypothetical `data_len == 0` guard become equivalent once the invariant holds. **MEMBER 1 (NULL test at :611) REJECTED as the fix** — symptom-only, leaves `data_len` lying for every future consumer, and **invents a second bespoke decline site THREE LINES ABOVE the existing #460 one**. It remains a valid ATTRIBUTION CONTROL, which is what it was built as. **MEMBER 3 (both) REJECTED** — the extra NULL test is licensed by neither a measured delta nor a transferred warrant. Its only residual concern, a future edit bypassing the tail, is a DETECTOR obligation under clause (b)/(d), not product code. Per the ADDITION LICENCE, *\"cannot hurt\" is how dead guards accrete*. No fourth member is needed. *** RANKING: TOP OF THE UN-STARTED QUEUE, BUT DO NOT PREEMPT `wdcflood`. *** A host SIGSEGV on nine legal accesses is MEMORY-UNSAFE and uncontrolled, which outranks even the controlled `exit(1)` items **because it is invisible to the exit-code oracle the battery leans on** — and far above a stderr flood. But `wdcflood` is mid-round with its table running, and the carrier is explicit that more witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger ones. This row is witnessed and filed; it loses nothing by waiting one round, whereas preemption discards settled work. **Sequence: finish `wdcflood`, then this.** One check when it starts: confirm `sh4chcr`'s held state before ranking against it — a SIGSEGV still outranks a controlled `exit(1)`, but verify the hold rather than assume it. *** WHAT THE SHIPPED DETECTOR OWES BEYOND THE 13-ROW WITNESS: *** (a) trigger rows red pre-fix / green post-fix — **already have it**. (b) **THE REINSTATEMENT ROW THE WITNESS DOES NOT CARRY**: because the fix is a single assignment, REMOVING `d->data_len = 0` verbatim reinstates the defect, and a row must redden under that. Buildable directly from the attribution artifacts. (d) the fix adds NO PREDICATE, so this is the DEPENDS-ON case: the mutant reroutes the EXISTING guard's repair, drawn from the enclosing function only. Two forms — **(i) relocate or scope `data_len = 0` so the :445-446 read-failure path is not covered, which reddens `cheap trig` and is the load-bearing one**; and (ii) operand-swap the surviving :611 guard to a body-read sibling such as `d->cnt`. **Because member 2 drives ALL length fields to 0 in the NULL state, some operand swaps will measure EQUIVALENT — record that as a RESULT under the won't-die rule, not as a gap.** (accept) a successful PIO read must still return real device bytes: **the existing `devsig gd0`/`gd1` rows (0x0010 / 0xffff9600) ARE that row** — just confirm they run against the FIXED build's success path, proving member 2 did not over-zero `data_len` on the good path; an unconditional `data_len = 0` reddens them. (e) the header names what it catches: removal or relocation of `data_len = 0`, guard-operand swap at :611, reintroduction of free-without-reset. **(f) ANCHOR RULE: N/A — this is a NULL-deref detector, not a decode-position one, and makes no claim about WHERE an access lands. State that in the contract so a later reader does not read the absence as a gap.** *** PROVISIONALITY, STATED BY THE ADJUDICATOR ITSELF: the DEFECT is fully panel-vetted (all nine seats reviewed it 2026-08-24 plus the opus5 rung-3 measurement), but THE OPTION-SET RULING IS A RECOMMENDATION TO CARRY INTO PASS 1, NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR IT. *** Pass 1 must still test the tail-else form against the seats with one specific question: **does routing into the #460 decline path change any guest-visible behaviour on the SUCCESS path?** That is the single thing the adjudication reasoned rather than measured.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-20460-   "sev": "high",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-20461-   "note": "*** CLOSED 2026-08-30.  The PIO NULL deref is dead in practice, and the closure buries nothing. ***  The guard is a LENGTH test (`cur_data_offset < data_len`), not a NULL test -- so the closure rests on the pairing, and the pairing was checked rather than assumed: `d->data` and `d->data_len` are assigned at exactly FOUR lines (alloc sets both, `free_data()` clears both) with **no third writer**.  **The transient window is representable and UNOBSERVABLE.**  `alloc_data()` sets the length before the pointer, so a disagreeing state exists between two C statements -- but `CHECK_ALLOCATION` exits the process on failure, and device dispatch is synchronous inside the guest's own access, so no guest load can interleave.  *The round-2 seat that corrected an earlier 'UNREPRESENTABLE' overclaim was right; the honest statement is unobservable, not impossible.*  **The future-edit hazard has a MEASURED kill**: `Md_nolen` (helper kept, only `data_len = 0` deleted) dies at 10/13 and 7/9 against 13/13 and 9/9 bases.  **The conventional-pairing residual has its own carrier** (`gdromallocpair`, open), and the one-byte-guard/two-byte-read latency has `gdrompiooddlen`.  *** ONE L4 CORRECTED AT CLOSURE, FOUND BY THE REGRESS PHASE: the witness field said the killtable README records the det_ logs as 'the INTENT-ONLY 16-row table and not the full 26'.  FALSE since pass 2 -- `5b5089d` corrected the README, and the logs themselves now carry BOTH denominators (`FULL=16/26 INTENTONLY=10/16`).  The fix landed and the FIELD DESCRIBING IT did not: the sibling-grep class, caught by the phase that exists to catch it. ***"
