GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23412-   "opened": "2026-08-29",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23413-   "note": "*** MEASURED, NOT REASONED: `ps` showed `git -C ... status --porcelain` at 2:03 elapsed with NO verdict file and NO battery log on disk -- and the FULL span was ~15 MINUTES, not two: the task fired at 16:06:12 and the verdict's own START line reads 16:21:09. ***  (I filed 'over two minutes' from the single sample I had; the run's own record settled it afterwards. A number written from one observation is a lower bound, not a measurement.)  `nightly_battery.sh` computes `HEADSHA` and `DIRTY` BEFORE it opens either output: the verdict block ends `} > \"$SUM\"` and the run line is `./run.sh > \"$LOG\" 2>&1`, and a shell creates a redirect target only when it reaches that redirect.  So for the whole of the git call there is nothing to observe.  **THIS IS EXACTLY THE AMBIGUITY THE CARRIER'S OWN REDIRECT-TARGET RULE EXISTS TO RESOLVE, arriving inside the battery's own pre-flight.**  That rule says: log MISSING -> the command NEVER STARTED; log 0 bytes -> started.  Here the log is missing and the command HAS started, so the discriminator gives the wrong answer -- and it gives it during the exact window when an operator is most likely to be checking.  It compounds a hazard the script's own header already records: a battery launched from a Claude Code shell is ORPHANED, and *'the only tell was a log mtime 4.4 h stale'*.  During the blind window there is no mtime to read, so a genuinely orphaned start and a healthy one are INDISTINGUISHABLE by every documented check.  The only thing that separated them today was `ps -eo pid,ppid,etime,args`, which named the git child.  **REMEDY IS ONE LINE AND COSTS NOTHING: write a STARTING stamp to `$SUM` (or touch `$LOG`) BEFORE the git calls**, so the redirect-target rule works on this script.  The stamp is overwritten by the real verdict block moments later, so nothing is lost.  *Not fixed in the same pass because the battery was RUNNING and editing a shell script mid-execution is a documented defect class here -- bash reads incrementally by byte offset, and the failure looks like a syntax error in a file that parses clean afterwards.*  SECOND, SMALLER FINDING: the git call itself is slow because the repo is Windows-owned and WSL reaches it over /mnt/c.  Whether it should be narrowed (`git status --porcelain -- src/ tools/ regress/`) or simply stamped is a design question, not a defect -- **the blind window is the defect, and it exists at any duration.**  *** THE WINDOW'S DURATION IS TREE-STATE-DEPENDENT, measured the same evening on a SECOND run.  The defect is real and the ~15-minute figure is NOT its general size. ***  The 16:06 run took ~15 minutes to reach `START` because the pre-flight is `git status --porcelain` over `/mnt/c` on a DIRTY tree carrying a whole round's uncommitted work.  The 22:39 run reached `run.sh` in about SEVENTEEN SECONDS -- same machine, same command, same script -- because the tree was CLEAN.  **So the blind window is near-instant when it does not matter and long exactly when a round is mid-flight**, which is when someone is most likely to be watching it and most likely to misread the absence.  That makes it worse than a fixed-cost gap, not better: the failure mode is correlated with the moment of highest attention.  The remedy is unchanged and now has a second reason: a STARTING stamp written before the git calls costs nothing and removes a window whose length nobody can predict from the outside."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23414-  },
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23415-  {
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23416-   "id": "diskimagesign",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23417-   "title": "#472's clamp introduced two -Wsign-compare warnings that cost a GATE",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23418-   "state": "in work",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23419-   "witness": "detector:regress/artifacts/diskimagesign/cascade_20260829",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23420-   "opened": "2026-08-29",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:23421:   "note": "*** NOT A COSMETIC WARNING -- IT WAS COSTING AN ENTIRE GATE'S COVERAGE, and that was found only by re-running a battery that had been RED AND UNREAD FOR FIVE DAYS. ***  `#472` replaced `len > OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE? OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE : len` with `len > remain? remain : len`, where `len` is `size_t` (parameter) and `remain` is `off_t`.  gcc warns twice at diskimage.c:1382 under the tree's own CWARNINGS.  **THE CASCADE:** `gate_build` wants ZERO warnings, so it FAILED; it withdraws `$RIG/gxsec-gxemul` at start and republishes only on success; `gate_mips` then `need_exec`s that binary and reported *'SKIP -- missing or non-executable binary'*.  **A whole gate lost, scored as SKIP rather than FAIL** -- byte-identical in shape to what one stale allowlist line cost on 2026-08-23, which is the defect `gatebuildallow` records.  **IT IS NOT A LIVE BUG AND THE ROUND SAYS SO:** `inblock = curofs & (OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1)` gives [0,511], so `remain` is in [1,512] and positive by construction; the conversion is value-preserving at every reachable value.  Both casts are therefore provable, not plausible.  MEASURED, control first: the reconstructed pre-fix line compiles rc=0/44072 b with **2** sign-compare warnings; the shipped source compiles rc=0/44112 b with **0**.  *** MY FIRST VERIFICATION WAS VACUOUS AND ITS OWN CONTROL CAUGHT IT: *** it reported 0 warnings for the PRE-FIX form, which must be 2.  The compile was failing on a missing include and the script read its empty output as a result -- the phantom-absence class, in a script written to prove a fix.  Take 2 reports compile rc and object size BEFORE the warning count and refuses to read the count without them.  EXPOSURE, stated honestly: warnings need a BUILD, builds are too slow for precommit, so warnings are checked only by the weekly battery -- a warning-introducing commit is invisible for up to seven days, and only if someone reads the result.  *** THE ROW NAMED NO COMMIT WHILE THE FIX HAD SHIPPED.  Found 2026-08-30 by reading `src/disk/diskimage.c`: the clamp now reads `lentoread = len > (size_t) remain? remain : (off_t) len;` with a `#491` comment proving both casts safe -- `inblock` is `curofs` masked to [0, OVERLAY_BLOCK_SIZE-1], so `remain` is in [1, 512] and can never be negative. ***  `git log -S` puts it in **18893de**, now recorded.  **This is the same shape a gate caught on `holdmarkermulti` in the same session -- work shipped, row names no commit -- and it did NOT trip here only because the state is `in work` rather than `open`.**  `check_row_states` scopes its shipped-work test to open rows, so an `in work` row can carry shipped code indefinitely without any gate objecting.  *Recorded as an observation about the checker's scope, not filed as a defect: widening it to `in work` would fire on every round that is genuinely mid-flight, which is what `in work` means.  The honest fix is the habit of filling `commits` at ship time, not a wider gate.*  The row's own EXPOSURE note stands unchanged and is the important part: warnings need a BUILD, builds are too slow for precommit, so a warning-introducing commit is invisible for up to seven days -- and only then if someone reads the battery result.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23422-   "commits": "18893de"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23423-  },
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23424-  {
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23425-   "id": "propagatecpsilent",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23426-   "title": "The propagate script prints 'propagated' after a FAILED cp",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23427-   "state": "closed",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23428-   "witness": "harness:regress/artifacts/makercfalse/propagate_controls_20260830/ -- SIX checks in BOTH directions, sentinel CPGUARD_PASS.  The old idiom is reproduced and shown to announce success on a missing source; the shipped idiom is shown to report the failure AND to catch a case cp's exit status cannot see.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23429-   "opened": "2026-08-29",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-23430-   "note": "Measured 2026-08-29 on my own tooling.  The build-tree propagate script does `cp \"$SEC/$F\" \"$D/$F\"` and then unconditionally `echo \"  propagated\"`.  When the path was wrong the log read:  *cp: cannot stat '...': No such file or directory*  immediately followed by  *propagated*.  **That is a CLAIM OF ACTION printed at the exact moment of the failure**, the same shape as `panel.sh` printing *'re-firing once'* while re-firing nothing -- which this carrier already names as worse than a stale citation, because it reassures the one person who could still fix it.  **THE SENTINEL DISCIPLINE CONTAINED IT, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  The two-part clearance reported `part 1 source cmps clean: NO` and KEPT the sentinel, so the tree was marked untrustworthy rather than silently wrong.  The false line cost confusion, not a wrong measurement.  FIXED in the rewritten script (`cp ... || { echo 'cp FAILED'; continue; }`), but the ORIGINAL at the #490 script still carries it, and any future copy inherits it.  *Root cause worth naming: the bad script was GENERATED FROM ANOTHER SCRIPT BY CHAINED `sed`, which produced `src/devices/disk/diskimage.c` from `src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c`.  Generating a script by regex is the same class as substituting a grep for a parser -- it produces something that runs and is wrong.*",
--
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30647-  {
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30648-   "id": "queueunrated",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30649-   "sev": "med",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30650-   "state": "open",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30651-   "opened": "2026-09-07",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30652-   "title": "47% of the open queue carries NO severity, and 73 unrated rows are already WITNESSED -- so 'prioritise critical bugs' cannot see half the backlog",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30653-   "witness": "none",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30654-   "entries": [],
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30655-   "commits": [],
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30656:   "state_note": "Derived 2026-09-07 while trying to obey the standing directive *prioritise critical bugs and also\neasy resolvable ones*.  **The directive is unexecutable over half the queue.**\n\n    open + in-work        187\n    carrying `sev`        100   (high 10, med 40, low 50)\n    UNRATED                87   = 47%\n    unrated AND WITNESSED  73\n\n**The 73 is the number that matters.**  A witness makes a row ELIGIBLE under the ladder, so those are\nrows that could be worked today and that nothing has ranked.  Ranking is what the carrier says orders\nthe queue -- *rung is a floor on admissibility, never a priority; SEVERITY and gate blind-spots order\nthe queue* -- so an unranked witnessed row is invisible to the only ordering rule this project has.\n\n*** THE FIX IS NOT A REGEX, AND I NEARLY SHIPPED ONE. ***  A keyword scan over titles buckets 7 rows\nunder host-kill language, 5 under memory-safety and 17 under wrong-data.  **Those buckets are\nCORRELATES.**  What makes a row HIGH here is *a guest can reach it and something bad happens*, and no\nkeyword tests reachability -- which is the deriver rule (*key on what DEFINES the class, not on a\ncorrelate of it*) applied to my own triage script.  The scan is kept only as a READING ORDER.\n\n**Candidates the scan surfaced, recorded as UNVERIFIED reading order and explicitly NOT as gradings:**\n`gdromcondavail` (COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread), `asicindexcase` (a deleted\nguard that was also a bounds check), `gdrompiooddlen` (guards one byte, reads two), `asickillsurvive`\n(four kill rows survive-only, so an OOB read is unobserved), `gdromdmawidth` (a tail store ignoring\n`len`), `sh4pcic15` (an interrupt line that can never be delivered), `asicpermute` (an index\npermutation).  **Each needs its reachability read before any `sev` is written.**\n\n> **DISPOSITION: a RATING pass, evidence-first, one row at a time, with the reachability sentence\n> quoted from the source.**  It is read-only, so it parallelises freely and belongs to the file-reading\n> seats -- the exact work the standing directive says to give codex.  It is NOT a round: nothing is\n> edited, and the deliverable is `sev` plus a one-line warrant per row.\n\n*Related but distinct from [[baretitle]], which counts rows with an EMPTY NOTE.  A row can be richly\ndocumented and still unranked -- most of these 73 are.*\n\n*** MY '73 WITNESSED' FIGURE IS WRONG, AND IT IS THE CORRELATE TRAP FOR THE THIRD TIME TODAY. ***\ncodex: *\"**37 of those 73 have witness strings beginning `none`** -- sometimes followed by\nhistorical measurements, sometimes explicitly denying reachability.  **The snippet counts strings,\nnot demonstrated witnesses.**\"*\n\nMy test was `r.get('witness') and r.get('witness') != 'none'` -- which passes any string that merely\nSTARTS with `none`, and this ledger's convention is to write `none -- <why>` with the reasoning\nattached.  **So the honest figure is about 36 witnessed-and-unrated rows, not 73.**\n\n*The paragraph above this one warns against keying on a correlate, and the snippet printed\nimmediately below it does exactly that.  Re-derive with a prefix test, and note that `none -- ...`\nrows are often the BEST documented ones, since a denial of reachability takes more evidence than an\nassertion of it.*\n\n*** A PATTERN IN MY OWN METHOD, WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE IT IS NOW FOUR FOR FOUR TODAY: EVERY\nCENSUS I RAN KEYED ON ONE SPELLING AND MISSED A VARIANT. ***\n\n    severity          the ledger's field is `sev`              -> 0 of 260 rows matched\n    witness != 'none' the convention is `none -- <why>`        -> 37 of 73 miscounted as witnessed\n    << 24             uint16_t << 16 is the same defect        -> the structural site missed entirely\n    (int)idata        the tree also writes `(int) idata`       -> 226 where the answer is 309\n\n**Each was found by someone else running a different derivation, never by me re-reading mine.**  The\ncarrier's rule says *when two derivers disagree, neither is trustworthy until you look at what each\nMATCHED* -- but all four of these had only ONE deriver, so there was nothing to disagree with, and a\nsingle method produces a confident number with no signal attached.\n\n> **THE PRACTICE THAT FOLLOWS: a census that will be written into a durable record runs TWO\n> derivations with different keys, and prints BOTH.**  Agreement is weak evidence (they can share a\n> blindness -- `sgirekills` measured that) but DISAGREEMENT is a free, immediate signal, and it is\n> the only one available before a seat reads the work."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json-30657-  },
