gdromkeyindex: gdromkeyindex  five mutants score a full 32/32 against #461's detector, and one of them writes the DMA arming registers from the CONTROL window
WITNESS: none needed -- MEASURED detector escapes on shipped, above-floor material, i.e. coverage debt. The six closing rows were validated at 38/38 by the measuring seat, but the DIFF ITSELF IS GONE (its session scratchpad); the design is recorded in the entry and is sufficient to rebuild
opus5: 18 builds, 20 probe runs, against source byte-identical to ba30e64. FIVE new 32/32 escapes. FILED not fixed: the stopping rule reserves in-round fixes for a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and #461's detector is above the vacuity floor on all four clauses -- so these are coverage debt, the #447 disposition. *** E1 IS THE FINDING, AND IT DEFEATS THE ROUND'S STRUCTURAL DEFENCE BY GOING ROUND IT. *** Exchange the word- and bit-index expressions in gdrom_unimpl_first (`bits[k >> 5]` / `1u << (k & 31)` transposed). MEASURED, twice, with a standalone probe: four DECLINED 32-bit READS in the CONTROL window write 1 into dma_reg[1] (destination), [2] (length), [3] (count) and [5] (enable) -- which is EXACTLY case 0x18's arming condition, reached from the wrong window entirely. bits is a uint32_t[2]; `bits[k & 31]` runs to index 31 and the struct puts dma_reg[0] six words past it. ASan is blind (member-to-member inside one malloc). *** THE BOUNDS GUARD STILL PASSES: k is still off/4 < nkeys. The round's defence protects the BOUND; E1 lives in the INDEX, and no row was on the index. *** Honest scope from the seat: it is one transposition, and each HALF dies alone (m_swapidx 31/32, m_swapbit 29/32) -- the rows measure key DISTINCTNESS, which a transposition preserves exactly, so the detector catches either half and misses the whole. E2 `|=` -> `=` (32/32): defeats once-per-offset on any REVISIT -- read +0x00, +0x04, +0x00 gives three lines, so a repeated register scan re-floods forever. Every existing row is blind because none revisits a word after touching another in the same bitmap word. E3 the alignment guard rewritten as `len != sizeof(uint32_t)` (32/32): re-opens the alias, MEASURED -- a 4-byte write at DMA+0x05 lands 0xffffffff in dma_reg[1] against the seeded 0x11223344. Every existing guard row uses a NARROW access, so width and alignment are indistinguishable to the detector. No SH4 instruction can issue a misaligned 32-bit store, but memory_rw can, and debugger_cmds.c:1353 is one caller. E4 both alignment ternaries in the DMA diagnostics inverted (32/32) -- the messages lie about dropped-vs-stored and no row reads the text. E5 `if (idata != 0)` -> `if (0)` at case 0x18 (32/32): the DMA arm can be present, correctly routed, and completely INERT. Outside #461's semantic class -- it belongs to whoever fixes gdromexitsix's DMA+0x18 pair, and it is the same gap kimi named as its M2. SIX CLOSING ROWS ARE WRITTEN AND VALIDATED at fix = 38/38, with names checked mechanically against the gate's `^$v  .*ok$` rule (all <= 12 chars, no dups, no prefix collisions): `re-report` kills E2; `xwindow` + `xwindow x1` kill E1; `dma putw` kills E3; `dma rd txt` + `dma wr txt` kill E4 at ZERO extra cost by reading existing transcripts. Gate pin 32 -> 38. *** THE DIFF IS NOT RETRIEVABLE, and this note claimed it was -- corrected 2026-08-24 after looking for it. *** It lived in the measuring seat's OWN session scratchpad, a different path from the main loop's, and that directory is gone. A CLAIM THAT AN ARTEFACT IS RETAINED IS A CLAIM A FUTURE ROUND WILL ACT ON, so it has to be checked before it is written -- the same class as a score claim naming a log that does not exist. What survives is the DESIGN, which is recorded above in full and is enough to rebuild from: `re-report` kills E2 (3 != 2); `xwindow` + `xwindow x1` kill E1 (0x11223345 != 0x11223344); `dma putw` kills E3 (0xffffffff != 0x11223344); `dma rd txt` + `dma wr txt` kill E4 (0 != 1 each) at zero extra cost by reading existing transcripts. Rebuilding is a short job; hunting the file is not. ALSO MEASURED EQUIVALENT (not escapes, worth recording so nobody rebuilds them): deleting the bounds guard entirely; narrowing `size_t k` to uint8_t; deleting the zero-fill; handing the macro a pointer. And two mutants are HARD BUILD ERRORS -- widening or narrowing the latch element type -- which is what refutes this round's own 'the obvious sanity check passes' warning.
gdromcondavail: gdromcondavail  COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread -- the device advertises data while returning 0xffff (split out of gdromcondlies)
WITNESS: none -- a state-consistency defect; its oracle is what the device ADVERTISES
grok: SPLIT OUT of gdromcondlies, which named two defects at once. Original record at OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:7575-7587, a residual of #460: once a guest reads past the buffer, COND_DATA_AVAIL stays SET while every subsequent read returns 0xffff, announced once. Pre-fix that inconsistency was unmissable because the same read ended the host -- so the de-escalation made the state defect QUIETER without removing it, which is the class de-escalation always creates. Clearing COND_DATA_AVAIL at exhaustion would repair it, and was deliberately out of scope for #460 because changing what a device ADVERTISES is a behaviour change with its own oracle. Does NOT join the BADVAL round: different class, different oracle, different fix design.
gdromdmaclear: gdromdmaclear  a decline path that CLEARS an already-pending DMA completion escapes all 52 rows -- nodata quiet pins one direction of a two-way transition
WITNESS: none -- coverage debt above the vacuity floor
fable5: THE ONE ESCAPE THE CAPPED ASK-AGAIN FOUND AND KEPT. On a decline path, clear an already-pending GD-ROM DMA completion (plausible as 'failure cleanup'). `nodata quiet` cannot see it: that row proves a decline does not SET the bit, and M8 measured that transition (clear->set); this is set->clear, and every session() forks a fresh emulator so nothing is pending when the row runs. A ROW CAN PIN ONE DIRECTION OF A TWO-WAY TRANSITION AND READ AS THOUGH IT PINNED BOTH. The proposed construction does NOT work as written -- the success arm does not free d->data, so a second trigger succeeds again rather than reaching the no-data arm. The CHEAP variant is the ARMING arm: after a successful DMA write +0x0c=0 to break the arming, trigger, and require the completion bit STILL SET. Three stores on a session that exists. FILED not fixed: the detector is above the floor, and a row added at that point would ship WITHOUT a measured kill -- adding an unmeasured row to satisfy a rule about rows is the vacuity the rule exists to prevent.
gdromdmaguard: gdromdmaguard  `count == 1` -> `count <= 1` accepts a (0,1) arming the model refuses -- one character, and arm halfarm cannot see it
WITNESS: none -- coverage debt above the vacuity floor
fable5: From the #462 pass-2 harvest. Both decline blocks and diagnostics survive; the only tested mismatch (0,0) still declines because enable==1 fails, so it stays green -- but (0,1) is then ACCEPTED and a transfer runs for a guest that programmed no blocks. `arm halfarm` does NOT catch it: that row uses (1,0), which `count <= 1` also declines. RELATED AND NOW MEASURED: the outward-widening family (`count && enable`) was closed in-round by `arm count2`/`arm enable2` after three seats found it independently on the ask-again; M10 kills them at 55/57. This row is the remaining <= variant, which those two rows do not cover.
gatebuildallow: gatebuildallow  gate_build.sh PINS its own twin-tree allowlist while est_divergence.txt exists to prevent exactly that -- one stale entry cost a RED weekly battery and a silently SKIPPED gate for five days
WITNESS: harness:the 2026-08-23 battery verdict and log, plus a direct comparison of the two allowlists.  Preserved at _scratchpad/nightly/verdict_20260823_180000.txt and battery_20260823_180000.log.
opus5: FOUND BY RUNNING THE DEAD-MAN SWITCH WHEN THE OWNER ASKED ABOUT CRON STATUS -- not by any
gate, and not by anyone reading the battery.  `nightly_check.sh` has been reporting
NIGHTLY_CHECK_FAIL for five days.

THE 2026-08-23 WEEKLY BATTERY WENT RED: RUN_SH_EXIT=1, VERDICT_TOKEN=REGRESS_FAIL,
passed 14 / failed 1 / skipped 1, on a DIRTY tree (`*** VERDICT DOES NOT DESCRIBE A
COMMIT ***`).  The Windows task's own LastTaskResult is 1 and has been since.

*** THE FAILING CHECK IS ONE LINE OF TEXT, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IN gate_build PASSED: ***
both trees built, 223 and 224 objects, binaries produced, zero errors, zero warnings.

    FAIL no stale allowlist entries    got=1 want=0
         STALE (listed as divergent, but the files now agree): promemul/arcbios.c

AND IT CASCADED, exactly as the carrier's serialisation note predicts: gate_build withdraws
the shared arc binary at gate start and republishes ONLY on success, so its failure left the
binary unpublished and **gate_mips SKIPPED** -- a whole gate's coverage lost, scored as SKIP
rather than as a failure.  One stale line cost two gates.

ALREADY FIXED IN THE TREE, by accident rather than by anyone chasing it: `867c697` (#481)
removed the arcbios entry.  So a re-run should be green -- but nothing re-ran it, and the
dead-man switch cannot tell 'fixed' from 'never looked at'.

*** THE DEFECT THAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS THE ONE WORTH A ROUND: gate_build.sh PINS ITS OWN
ALLOWLIST. ***  `tools/est_divergence.txt` was created precisely to stop that -- its own
header says 'This file exists because the allowlist used to be PINNED inside the checker and
went stale TWICE'.  `precommit_check.sh` reads the file.  **`gate_build.sh` does not --
`grep -n est_divergence regress/gate_build.sh` returns NOTHING** -- and its pinned copy is
what went stale.

The two disagree TODAY, measured:
    gate_build.sh DIVERGENT  (5)  devices/{Makefile.skel,autodev.c,dev_jazz.c,dev_ne2000.c}
                                  machines/machine_arc.c
    est_divergence.txt       (6)  the same five PLUS devices/.index

The `.index` gap looks benign today -- the 8/23 run reported 'no divergence outside the
documented set: 0', so `list_diffs` does not surface it -- but benign-by-luck is the state
the file was written to end, and the stale entry proves the pinned copy drifts.

FIX (not taken here, because changing a gate owes negative controls): make gate_build.sh
READ est_divergence.txt, as precommit_check.sh already does.  Controls owed in both
directions -- prove it fails on an injected stale entry, and prove it passes unchanged on
the real list.

*And a scheduling hazard worth its own sentence: the battery fires on a fixed schedule and
caught the tree MID-EDIT (five modified files, including the device being worked that
afternoon).  A verdict that does not describe a commit cannot be attributed to anything,
which is the one thing a weekly battery exists to do.*
opus5: *** THE FIX THIS ROW FILED WOULD HAVE REPRODUCED THE DEFECT IT FIXES.  Found by reading,
before any code was written. ***

The row said: make `gate_build.sh` READ `est_divergence.txt`, as `precommit_check.sh`
already does, and cited the two lists disagreeing by `devices/.index` as evidence of drift.
**The disagreement is CORRECT, because the two consumers cover DIFFERENT POPULATIONS.**

  gate_build.sh   compares the whole trees via `list_diffs()`, which is
                  `find . \( -name '*.c' -o -name '*.h' -o -name '*.cc' -o -name
                  '*.skel' \)`.  **`.index` matches none of those, so list_diffs can
                  NEVER produce it.**  The gate then checks for STALE entries -- listed
                  as divergent but not actually divergent.
  precommit_check iterates `$CHANGED` from git, filtered to `src/*`, and uses the list to
                  SKIP known-divergent files.  Any path can appear there, `.index`
                  included, because the population comes from git rather than from a
                  find with extension filters.

*** SO IF gate_build READ THE FILE VERBATIM, `.index` WOULD ENTER ITS EXPECTED SET, WHERE
`list_diffs` CANNOT PRODUCE IT -- AND ITS OWN STALE-ENTRY CHECK WOULD FLAG IT STALE ON
EVERY RUN.  Permanently red.  That is exactly the failure that cost five days and a
skipped gate, reintroduced by the repair for it. ***

THE ROW'S CORE FINDING SURVIVES AND IS UNCHANGED: gate_build pins its own list, that pinned
copy DID go stale, and the staleness cost a red battery plus `gate_mips`.  What is refuted
is only the proposed remedy and the `.index` evidence for it.

TWO CANDIDATE FIXES, neither taken here, both now stated with the constraint they must
satisfy:
  (a) gate_build reads the file but FILTERS to the extensions list_diffs covers, so the
      expected set and the actual set are drawn from the same population;
  (b) leave them separate and add a check that gate_build's list is exactly the
      extension-filtered SUBSET of the file's -- which catches drift without coupling two
      checks that legitimately differ.

*This is the addition licence doing its job before an edit rather than after: the proposed
change had no measured delta and no transferred warrant, only 'the two lists disagree, so
unify them'.  Reading what each list is FOR took minutes and refuted it.*

