GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline\matrix.html:433:    REQUEST SENSE.  **This also refutes the row&#x27;s own state_note, which says asc does notw -&gt; **2**; E state-contradicts-closure 30 raw -&gt; **5**. *** RULE 5, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT COST THE MOST: `closure.note` MAY DISPOSE OF A STAGE, NOT THE ROW. *** The ledger defines four `phase_labels` -- assess, research, review, regress -- so `CLOSE (assess)` closes the ASSESS STAGE and the row legitimately stays `held`. TWENTY-FIVE of class E&#x27;s thirty are that shape; `exitsweep`&#x27;s own hold says so out loud (*&quot;CLOSURE REVERTED ... the fable5 batch answered this row&#x27;s held stage&quot;*). Reading a stage-scoped disposition as a row-scoped one manufactured 25 false defects. THE TWELVE THAT SURVIVE: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmadefault, witnessinvisible, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m437ord, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, cmtattr, hpcshcpu -- plus this row. Note the convergence worth trusting: `fbwitness`, `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` were named INDEPENDENTLY by the reading seats AND fall out of the mechanical census, which is the only kind of agreement that counts here.  RECONCILIATION, demanded by the adjudicator before either number may be quoted: the class column sums to **13 instances** and the repair set is **11 rows**. 13 - 1 dedup (`fbwitness` is in BOTH closed-no-witness and closed-no-commits) - 1 self (`ledgerstatelies` is this tracking row) = 11. Both numbers are correct; the arithmetic was simply never shown, which made the correction READ as contradicting its own column -- **a sixth counting defect sitting inside the correction that reports the fifth.**  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S CENTRAL FINDING, MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT WHAT EITHER SEAT CLAIMED: `state` IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE RECORD. *** Two file-reading seats disagreed on `fbwitness` -- one said `CLOSE (assess)` disposes of a STAGE so the row must be `held`, the other said it retires the assessment so `closed` is fine. Settled empirically rather than by vote, and BOTH inferences are unsupported: **23 rows carry the IDENTICAL `closure.note` of `CLOSE (assess)` and they are spread across THREE states -- 12 `held`, 7 `in work`, 4 `closed`** (the closed four being ns16550, fablequeue, carrier, fbwitness). The obvious second hypothesis was tested and also fails: the number of STAGES with recorded entries is statistically identical across the three groups (mean 1.8 / 1.8 / 1.9, same min and max), so stage coverage does not explain the split either. **`state` and `closure.note` are independent fields with no enforced relationship**, so a reader cannot derive one from the other and neither can a checker. That is why &#x27;DO NOT TRUST STATES&#x27; is the correct verdict, and it reframes this round: **the repair cannot be a mechanical rewrite of 11 cells, because there is no rule to rewrite them TO.** The round must first establish what `state` MEANS -- most plausibly &#x27;is there work outstanding on this row&#x27;, which is a different question from &#x27;has any stage been adjudicated&#x27; -- and only then repair. ALSO REFUTED HERE, and worth keeping because it was checkable in two minutes: the claim that `fbwitness` is ALONE among its 2026-08-20 cohort in being closed. That cohort is 84 rows -- 17 closed, 43 held, 11 dropped, 13 in work -- and four of the closed share `fbwitness`&#x27;s exact closure string. A seat&#x27;s argument from &#x27;this row is the only one&#x27; is worth checking every time; this one was wrong.  *** FIVE SEATS, ELEVEN ROWS, AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS THE EVIDENCE RATHER THAN THE NOISE. *** agy, kimi-k3, glm-5.2, minimax-m3 and grok each assigned a correct state to all eleven rows, reading the same ledger under the same four validity rules stated in the brief. **They agree on only 4 of 11.** `hpcshcpu` drew THREE different answers (closed / dropped / held); six more drew two. This is what a field with no derivable meaning looks like from the outside: competent independent readers do not converge, because there is nothing to converge ON. *** IT ALSO PARTITIONS THE ROUND CLEANLY, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. *** **UNANIMOUS (4) -- and all four are ALREADY IN THE RIGHT STATE**: `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` (closed, missing `commits`), `m437ord` (closed, missing `closure.by`), `cmtattr` (held, correct as-is). What these need is FIELDS, not state changes, and a field that is absent is absent under any definition of `state` -- so they are repairable NOW, independently of the rule question. **SPLIT (7)**: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, hpcshcpu. Every one of these turns on what `state` MEANS, and none can be repaired until that is settled -- editing them now would be manufacturing the appearance of correctness by picking whichever seat I found most persuasive. **THE SPLIT IS ALSO A CHECK ON THE 23-ROW FINDING**, arrived at from the opposite direction: that one measured one closure string against three states; this one measures five readers against one row set. Both say the same thing and neither depends on the other&#x27;s method.  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25, AND IT REFRAMED THE ROUND WHILE CONVICTING ME OF ONE OVERREACH. *** The ruling: `state` and `closure.note` are different TENSES. The note is a PAST-TENSE event log of what happened to a STAGE; `state` is a PRESENT-TENSE assertion of what the ROW still OWES. **Under that reading the multi-state spread of one closure string is the ledger WORKING AS DESIGNED, not the defect** -- rows that experienced the same stage event legitimately differ in what else they owe -- and the stage-coverage test failing is CORROBORATION rather than a second mystery, because state depends on what REMAINS, not on how much happened. **SO THE THING TO FILE IS THE MISSING SCHEMA SEMANTICS, NOT THE SPREAD.** That single absence is the cause behind six mis-counts in one session, two seats inferring opposite conventions from one string, and five seats agreeing on only 4 of 11 rows. The fix is written: a `state_semantics` block now sits in the ledger&#x27;s own schema, PENDING OWNER RATIFICATION, forward-only in the same way `witness` was. *** MY OVERREACH, RETRACTED IN PLACE: I wrote that &#x27;for most of the 11 there is no fact of the matter to restore -- only a decision&#x27;. THAT IS FALSE AND IT WAS CONVENIENT FOR ME*** -- it converts eleven tedious substance reads into one interesting rule question. What died was a repair METHOD (derive state from the note), not the fact of the matter, which is read from commits, gates, holds and filed questions -- exactly how `asicbadaddr` was closed, with no note convention needed. **The seminar is over in one paragraph; the eleven reads remain owed.** *** AND NO NUMBER IN THIS ROW MAY BE QUOTED AS A PREMISE UNTIL A SECOND PARTY CHECKS IT. *** I have mis-counted this ledger six times in one session. Every ruling above depends on the EXISTENCE of the spread, not its size -- ONE held row and ONE closed row sharing a closure string already prove non-derivability -- so the whole second-party check is two greps. The figures 23, 84 and the 1.8/1.8/1.9 stage means are UNVERIFIED SUPPORTING COLOUR, labelled as such, and are leads rather than premises.  *** A SEVENTH COUNTING HAZARD, FOUND BY MAKING IT: SEAT NAMES ARE NOT SEAT ANSWERS. *** While closing #463&#x27;s stages I reported research at 9/9; it was 8/9. The `codex` entry on that stage is a **`[HELD AWAITING SEAT:` MARKER** -- a record that the seat is OUTSTANDING -- and I counted it because I counted seat NAMES. `check_stage_panels.py` excludes marker entries deliberately, and its own comment says exactly why: *&#x27;otherwise marking a gap would close it.&#x27;* **Writing down that a seat is missing would, under a name-count, make the stage look complete** -- the seat-failure-as-agreement shape in its purest mechanical form. **THE LEDGER CARRIES 44 SUCH MARKER ENTRIES**, each one a seat that has NOT answered. Any future census of panel coverage that counts names rather than answers over-reports by up to that much. Three marker tokens exist and all three must be excluded: `[HELD AWAITING SEAT:`, `[NOT A PANEL STAGE: SINGLE-SEAT RE-READ]`, and `[RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY]`. *This belongs with the schema-semantics finding rather than beside it: `entries` is not a list of answers, it is a list of EVENTS, and some events record an absence. That is the same present-tense/past-tense confusion that `state` and `closure.note` embody, one level down.*  *** A NEW INSTANCE OF THIS ROW&#x27;S OWN DEFECT, FOUND 2026-08-25 BY THE ADJUDICATOR TRIPPING OVER IT WHILE ADJUDICATING THIS VERY BLOCK. *** Asked to rule on the queue, the fable5 seat reported that `idesync` &quot;is already closed as document-do-not-fix&quot;. The ledger says `state = &quot;in work&quot;` with `commits` EMPTY. Both readings are half right, and the shape is new: *** THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BUT THE DOCUMENT HAS NOT SHIPPED. *** The row carries an opus5 REVIEW verdict (2026-08-20) of DOCUMENT-DO-NOT-FIX on a deciding fact nobody else raised -- the drive never advertises FLUSH CACHE, so the highest IDENTIFY word written never sets it -- plus a closed assess stage (`closure.by = fable5`, `CLOSE (assess)`). A SUBSTANCE reader therefore sees a finished decision and says &quot;closed&quot;; a STATE reader sees no commits and says &quot;unfinished&quot;. Under the semantics now written, `closed` means discharged BY WORK, and a documenting commit IS work that has not happened -- so the state field is RIGHT and the adjudicator was WRONG, while the adjudicator was right about the only thing anyone would act on. *** THE LESSON IS THAT `document-do-not-fix` NEEDS ITS OWN DISCHARGE, AND NOTHING TRACKS IT. *** A row in this state is invisible to both readings: it looks decided to anyone reading the verdict and claimed to anyone reading the state, so nobody ships the document. That is an UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION with no carrier -- the class section I of precommit exists for, one field over. Candidate fix, NOT applied pending ratification: `closed` requires either a commit or an explicit `documented-only` closure badge, so an assessed-not-changed record cannot close without the record existing. The project&#x27;s ethos already demands honest &#x27;assessed, not changed&#x27; records; what is missing is anything that NOTICES when one was promised and never written.</span></span><span class="state st-open" title="THIS IS ITS OWN ROUND. The matrix panel below is its PASS-1. Adjudicated 2026-08-25: #463 may not repair it -- ~50 instances across five classes is a records-correction round, and the standing rule for those (budget a SECOND review pass, because corrections repeat the errors they correct) cannot be honoured inside another round.">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span